'''bay-winged''' (bā′wingd), ''a.'' Having chestnut color on the wings. —'''Bay-winged bunting,''' {{smaller|the grass-finch or vesper-bird, ''Poœcetes gramineus'', one of the commonest sparrows of North America.}} —'''Bay-winged longspur,''' {{smaller|''Rhynchophanes maccowni'', a common fringilline bird of the western prairies, related to the Lapland longspur.}}—'''Bay-winged summer-finch,''' {{smaller|''Peucœa carpalis'' of Arizona.}}

'''bay-wood''' (bā′wu̇d), ''n.'' [< ''bay''<sup>2</sup> + ''wood''<sup>1</sup>.] The lighter and coarser kind of mahogany, coming especially from British Honduras. See ''mahogany''.

'''bay-yarn''' (bā′yärn), ''n.'' [< ''bay''<sup>6</sup>, ''a.'', + ''yarn''.] Woolen yarn used in the manufacture of baize.

'''baza''' (bä′zä̤), ''n.'' [E. Ind.; cf. Ar. ''bāz'', a hawk.] In ''ornith.'', the name of an East Indian kite. It is also used as a generic name.

'''bazaar, bazar''' (ba̤-zär′), ''n.'' [Formerly also ''basar'', ''buzzar'', ''bussar'', also ''bazarro'' (cf. It. ''baz-''

